    "q-36714": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36714",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+bx+3\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ exists. What is the value of the constant $b?$",
      "worked_solution": "In order for ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ to exist, the left and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n- Approaching from the left, we have $x<2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-1.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{x}^{2}+x-1 \\\\  & =(2)^{2}+2-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Approaching from the right, we have $x>2,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}+bx+3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{x}^{2}+bx+3 \\\\  & =(2)^{2}+b\\cdot 2+3 \\\\  & =7+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 5 & =7+2b \\\\ 2b & =-2 \\\\ b & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
